1
00:00:05,336 --> 00:00:11,194
Jim Burden grows up on the Nebraska prairie, leaves for the city and spends his life missing it.

2
00:00:11,563 --> 00:00:15,094
This guide covers the novel and a version where he stays.

3
00:00:16,184 --> 00:00:20,039
Hello, and welcome to Unwritten Chapters, Study Edition.

4
00:00:20,264 --> 00:00:24,644
In the next few minutes, I'll walk you through My Ántonia, by Willa Cather.

5
00:00:24,896 --> 00:00:29,374
It's a quick guide you can use for class, a book club, or your own curiosity.

6
00:00:29,606 --> 00:00:33,649
The full guide, with ten museum and archive sources, is linked below.

7
00:00:35,799 --> 00:00:41,823
Ten-year-old Jim Burden, an orphan from Virginia, comes to live with his grandparents in Nebraska.

8
00:00:42,184 --> 00:00:48,787
On the same train is the Shimerda family, newly arrived from Bohemia, including a girl named Ántonia.

9
00:00:49,307 --> 00:00:51,649
Jim teaches Ántonia English.

10
00:00:51,826 --> 00:00:55,720
Her family struggles through hard winters, and her father dies.

11
00:00:55,933 --> 00:01:02,032
As they grow up, Ántonia works in the fields and later as a hired girl in the town of Black Hawk.

12
00:01:02,475 --> 00:01:06,998
Jim leaves for university and becomes a railroad lawyer in New York.

13
00:01:07,344 --> 00:01:16,021
Twenty years later he visits Ántonia, now married with many children on her own farm, and realises they share a past nobody

14
00:01:16,021 --> 00:01:17,682
else can understand.

15
00:01:18,304 --> 00:01:21,967
Next, who's who, so you can keep everyone straight.

16
00:01:23,726 --> 00:01:24,804
Jim Burden.

17
00:01:24,942 --> 00:01:28,735
The narrator, a thoughtful boy who becomes a lawyer in the book.

18
00:01:29,337 --> 00:01:30,906
Ántonia Shimerda.

19
00:01:31,161 --> 00:01:34,018
A warm, strong girl from Bohemia.

20
00:01:34,478 --> 00:01:36,786
Grandmother and Grandfather Burden.

21
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:40,024
Jim's kind, hardworking grandparents.

22
00:01:40,782 --> 00:01:42,153
Anton Cuzak.

23
00:01:42,402 --> 00:01:45,749
The cheerful Bohemian farmer Ántonia marries.

24
00:01:47,885 --> 00:01:50,010
Now for the story.

25
00:01:50,190 --> 00:01:53,253
Here's how it unfolds, chapter by chapter.

26
00:01:53,619 --> 00:01:54,740
Chapter one.

27
00:01:54,966 --> 00:01:56,574
The Edge of the World.

28
00:01:57,431 --> 00:02:00,964
Jim Burden was ten years old when he came to Nebraska.

29
00:02:01,398 --> 00:02:05,684
His mother and father had both died that year, back home in Virginia.

30
00:02:06,057 --> 00:02:12,821
He was sent west on the train to live with his grandparents, who had a farm out on the prairie near a little town called Black

31
00:02:12,821 --> 00:02:13,339
Hawk.

32
00:02:13,721 --> 00:02:18,490
He travelled with a young farmhand named Jake, who was going west to work for them.

33
00:02:18,875 --> 00:02:24,488
The trip took days and days, and Jim read a book about Jesse James the whole way.

34
00:02:24,916 --> 00:02:32,158
In the crowded car up front, on the same train, was a family who'd come all the way across the ocean from Bohemia.

35
00:02:32,431 --> 00:02:34,022
They spoke no English.

36
00:02:34,277 --> 00:02:37,933
There was a mother and father, a big son and a little sister.

37
00:02:38,189 --> 00:02:45,331
There was also a girl of about fourteen, with big warm eyes and brown cheeks, who looked at Jim very curiously.

38
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:47,235
Her name was Ántonia.

39
00:02:48,016 --> 00:02:49,134
Chapter two.

40
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:50,410
The Pumpkins.

41
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:57,048
Jim's grandparents' farm was a sturdy wooden house set down in the middle of all that grass.

42
00:02:57,341 --> 00:03:00,595
It had a garden behind it and a big cottonwood tree.

43
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:08,630
One afternoon soon after he arrived, his grandmother went out to dig potatoes in the garden, and Jim went with her.

44
00:03:08,990 --> 00:03:14,068
The garden was a long way from the house, at the end of a path through the tall grass.

45
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:18,559
When they got there, she told him he could stay out and rest a while if he liked.

46
00:03:18,816 --> 00:03:20,740
Then she walked back to the house.

47
00:03:21,273 --> 00:03:27,615
Jim sat down in the warm dirt in the middle of the garden and leaned back against a big yellow pumpkin.

48
00:03:28,552 --> 00:03:29,764
Chapter three.

49
00:03:29,946 --> 00:03:31,201
The Schoolhouse.

50
00:03:31,535 --> 00:03:35,785
Tonight, when the time came for Jim to leave, he didn't go.

51
00:03:36,295 --> 00:03:40,288
He was eighteen and had finished at the little high school in Black Hawk.

52
00:03:40,678 --> 00:03:42,453
Everyone said he was clever.

53
00:03:42,717 --> 00:03:48,883
They said he ought to go to the university in Lincoln and then on to the East, and make something of himself.

54
00:03:49,337 --> 00:03:52,972
His grandparents had saved the money, and the train ticket was bought.

55
00:03:54,066 --> 00:04:01,401
On the last evening, Jim walked out alone, past the garden and the cottonwood tree, into the tall grass.

56
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:03,921
He stood there as the sun went down.

57
00:04:04,558 --> 00:04:07,603
The whole prairie was turning red and gold.

58
00:04:07,861 --> 00:04:15,214
The grass moved in long, slow waves all the way to the edge of the sky, and a meadowlark was singing somewhere.

59
00:04:15,635 --> 00:04:20,860
Jim thought about the city, with its stone streets and crowded rooms and noise.

60
00:04:21,169 --> 00:04:24,203
He thought about the long years of remembering this.

61
00:04:24,625 --> 00:04:26,032
The Last Page.

62
00:04:26,837 --> 00:04:32,743
One evening in late summer, Jim was walking home across the prairie from the Cuzaks' farm.

63
00:04:33,087 --> 00:04:36,278
He'd eaten supper there with the whole noisy family.

64
00:04:36,832 --> 00:04:41,635
The sun was going down, huge and red, right at the edge of the world.

65
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:46,674
As he walked, he saw something on a rise in the distance that made him stop.

66
00:04:47,488 --> 00:04:53,076
Someone had left a plough standing in a field, and the sun was sinking exactly behind it.

67
00:04:53,237 --> 00:05:00,216
The plough stood out black against the red, with its handles, blade and long beam all sharp and clear.

68
00:05:00,366 --> 00:05:06,615
In the strange flat light it looked enormous, like a giant tool written on the face of the sun.

69
00:05:06,992 --> 00:05:11,456
Tonight, Jim stood in the grass and watched it until the sun was gone.

70
00:05:11,764 --> 00:05:19,213
Then he walked on home in the dusk, with the crickets starting up and the first stars coming out, the way he'd done ten thousand

71
00:05:19,213 --> 00:05:20,219
times before.

72
00:05:20,839 --> 00:05:24,948
Next, the big ideas, the ones that come up in class.

73
00:05:27,372 --> 00:05:30,823
Memory. Most of the book is Jim remembering.

74
00:05:30,982 --> 00:05:34,432
Our story asks what happens if he doesn't have to.

75
00:05:35,171 --> 00:05:36,389
Immigrant life.

76
00:05:36,604 --> 00:05:41,727
Cather shows families from many countries building new lives on hard land.

77
00:05:42,583 --> 00:05:44,219
Land and belonging.

78
00:05:44,538 --> 00:05:47,171
The prairie shapes everyone who lives on it.

79
00:05:48,120 --> 00:05:49,807
Ordinary heroism.

80
00:05:50,197 --> 00:05:54,530
Ántonia's courage is in daily work, not big adventures.

81
00:05:55,150 --> 00:05:58,966
Next, the exact moment our version changes the book.

82
00:06:01,356 --> 00:06:02,758
So what's the what-if?

83
00:06:02,912 --> 00:06:05,088
Here's the turning point we changed.

84
00:06:05,828 --> 00:06:09,765
In our story, Jim gives up his train ticket and stays.

85
00:06:09,951 --> 00:06:16,813
He becomes the teacher at the little sod schoolhouse, teaching immigrant children English the way he once taught Ántonia.

86
00:06:17,253 --> 00:06:23,218
Everything after that moment is new, but the characters still act the way their authors wrote them.

87
00:06:25,371 --> 00:06:28,455
Before we go, here's a little about who wrote it.

88
00:06:29,390 --> 00:06:34,658
Willa Cather moved from Virginia to Nebraska when she was nine, just like Jim.

89
00:06:34,808 --> 00:06:40,329
She grew up near Red Cloud among immigrant families from Bohemia, Sweden and Germany.

90
00:06:40,607 --> 00:06:45,340
She became a journalist and editor in New York, and later won the Pulitzer Prize.

91
00:06:45,859 --> 00:06:49,921
Ántonia was inspired by a real friend, Annie Pavelka.

92
00:06:51,943 --> 00:06:58,276
In the 1880s, the Homestead Act let families claim prairie land if they lived on it and farmed it.

93
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:02,992
Many settlers built houses of sod because there were almost no trees.

94
00:07:03,289 --> 00:07:08,228
Winters were brutal, and many families arrived knowing no English at all.

95
00:07:10,448 --> 00:07:11,859
Want to see the real thing?

